1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,399
When America Crashed, Singapore Sank:

2
00:00:02,479 --> 00:00:06,500
The Great Depression and the
Contagion That Still Reaches Us

3
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:09,657
In 1929 a pound of Malayan rubber

4
00:00:09,737 --> 00:00:12,875
sold for an average of 34 cents.

5
00:00:12,875 --> 00:00:16,771
By June 1932 it sold for 4.95 cents,

6
00:00:16,851 --> 00:00:20,275
the lowest price ever recorded.

7
00:00:20,275 --> 00:00:24,308
Singapore did not grow rubber,
but it lived by processing,

8
00:00:24,388 --> 00:00:28,918
financing and shipping the rubber
and tin of the Malay Peninsula,

9
00:00:28,998 --> 00:00:32,961
and much of both went to the
United States, where the car

10
00:00:33,041 --> 00:00:37,225
industry had collapsed after
the Wall Street crash of 1929.

11
00:00:37,225 --> 00:00:40,641
Singapore's later recessions
have arrived by other

12
00:00:40,721 --> 00:00:44,977
routes as well, and setting them
side by side shows a pattern:

13
00:00:45,057 --> 00:00:47,984
a crash abroad does
the most damage when it

14
00:00:48,064 --> 00:00:51,700
cuts demand for the goods
Singapore depends on most.

15
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:54,917
That pattern is worth
knowing today, when a large

16
00:00:54,997 --> 00:00:58,214
part of the island's growth
comes from making the

17
00:00:58,294 --> 00:01:01,725
chips and equipment behind
artificial intelligence.

18
00:01:01,725 --> 00:01:04,938
Much of Malaya's rubber
passed through Singapore

19
00:01:05,018 --> 00:01:07,900
to be graded, packed, insured and shipped.

20
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:12,475
Tin from the mines of Perak and
Selangor was smelted on Pulau Brani,

21
00:01:12,555 --> 00:01:16,800
an island off Singapore's harbour,
and shipped from there too.

22
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:21,310
According to the National Library
Board's account of the Depression,

23
00:01:21,390 --> 00:01:24,752
Singapore was "dependent
on international trade and

24
00:01:24,832 --> 00:01:28,950
the export of Malayan rubber
and tin to the American market".

25
00:01:28,950 --> 00:01:33,017
When the New York stock market
crashed in October 1929,

26
00:01:33,097 --> 00:01:38,300
the American economy went into a
slump, and the car industry with it.

27
00:01:38,300 --> 00:01:40,385
Fewer cars meant fewer tyres,

28
00:01:40,465 --> 00:01:43,975
and the price of rubber
fell month after month.

29
00:01:43,975 --> 00:01:49,815
On the 2nd of April 1931 the Straits
Times ran the headline "Still Falling:

30
00:01:49,895 --> 00:01:54,000
New Low Record for Rubber"
across the top of a page.

31
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:58,833
The price in London had fallen to
three and a quarter pence a pound,

32
00:01:58,913 --> 00:02:02,734
against an average of ten
and a quarter pence in 1929,

33
00:02:02,814 --> 00:02:06,130
and the paper gave the
reasons as large stocks,

34
00:02:06,210 --> 00:02:10,400
insufficient demand and
"continued depression in America."

35
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:15,475
A Singapore broker told the paper
he was "resigned to anything now."

36
00:02:15,475 --> 00:02:18,425
Price and volume fell together.

37
00:02:18,425 --> 00:02:22,781
Between 1929 and 1932,
the value of Singapore's

38
00:02:22,861 --> 00:02:26,367
rubber exports fell by 84 per cent and

39
00:02:26,447 --> 00:02:30,600
the value of its tin
exports by 68 per cent.

40
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:32,596
By the middle of 1930,

41
00:02:32,676 --> 00:02:36,450
about 500 shops in Singapore had closed.

42
00:02:36,450 --> 00:02:40,150
The slump then moved from goods to jobs.

43
00:02:40,150 --> 00:02:42,782
Estates and mines laid off workers,

44
00:02:42,862 --> 00:02:47,125
and the colony began sending
unemployed labourers home.

45
00:02:47,125 --> 00:02:50,246
Recruiting of labourers in India was

46
00:02:50,326 --> 00:02:53,350
stopped on the 1st of August 1930.

47
00:02:53,350 --> 00:02:58,102
In the Federated Malay States, the
government gave free passages to

48
00:02:58,182 --> 00:03:02,863
China to unemployed Chinese
labourers, and in May 1932 the Straits

49
00:03:02,943 --> 00:03:07,775
Times reported that three thousand
had left Perak in a single week.

50
00:03:07,775 --> 00:03:10,845
From August 1930 a quota limited the

51
00:03:10,925 --> 00:03:14,250
number of Chinese men allowed to land.

52
00:03:14,250 --> 00:03:19,890
Chinese male arrivals fell from 242,139

53
00:03:19,970 --> 00:03:23,703
in 1930 to 27,796 in 1933,

54
00:03:23,783 --> 00:03:29,422
and between 1931 and 1933 about 443,000

55
00:03:29,502 --> 00:03:35,075
more Chinese left Malaya than arrived.

56
00:03:35,075 --> 00:03:37,025
The banks were next.

57
00:03:37,025 --> 00:03:41,882
Many customers of the Chinese banks
were rubber planters and dealers,

58
00:03:41,962 --> 00:03:45,389
and in 1932 three of the
banks merged to survive,

59
00:03:45,469 --> 00:03:51,050
forming the Oversea-Chinese Banking
Corporation, the story of an earlier post.

60
00:03:51,050 --> 00:03:54,800
The slump eased only
when prices recovered.

61
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:58,439
In July 1933 the Governor,
Sir Cecil Clementi,

62
00:03:58,519 --> 00:04:03,693
declared that "the worst of the
slump is over," and rising rubber

63
00:04:03,773 --> 00:04:08,300
and tin prices brought
immigrants back in the mid-1930s.

64
00:04:08,300 --> 00:04:13,250
The chain in 1929 to 1932 ran in
one direction: a crash abroad,

65
00:04:13,330 --> 00:04:16,923
then a fall in demand
for the one or two goods

66
00:04:17,003 --> 00:04:22,033
Singapore depended on, then prices
and volumes falling together,

67
00:04:22,113 --> 00:04:26,025
then jobs, migrant workers
and finally the banks.

68
00:04:26,025 --> 00:04:28,610
Singapore's economy has shrunk,

69
00:04:28,690 --> 00:04:33,075
or nearly stopped growing,
in six years since 1961.

70
00:04:33,075 --> 00:04:37,430
Official national accounts
for Singapore begin in 1960,

71
00:04:37,510 --> 00:04:42,025
so there are no comparable
yearly figures for the 1930s.

72
00:04:42,025 --> 00:04:45,605
The chart shows each year's
growth in real terms,

73
00:04:45,685 --> 00:04:48,150
with the years of decline in red.

74
00:04:48,150 --> 00:04:52,841
The figures by industry show where
each of the later crises hit,

75
00:04:52,921 --> 00:04:56,275
and they do not all
follow the 1930s pattern.

76
00:04:56,275 --> 00:05:00,110
The chart below measures each
industry's contribution to

77
00:05:00,190 --> 00:05:02,836
the year's growth in percentage points,

78
00:05:02,916 --> 00:05:07,950
which depends both on how far the
industry fell and on how large it was.

79
00:05:07,950 --> 00:05:12,000
The bottom row shows the
growth of the whole economy.

80
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,475
1985.

81
00:05:14,475 --> 00:05:17,088
Singapore's first recession after

82
00:05:17,168 --> 00:05:20,025
independence was largely home-made.

83
00:05:20,025 --> 00:05:25,046
According to the National Library
Board, a construction boom had ended,

84
00:05:25,126 --> 00:05:29,428
with the market saturated by
the end of 1984, and high costs,

85
00:05:29,508 --> 00:05:34,250
together with a slowdown in the
United States, hurt manufacturing.

86
00:05:34,250 --> 00:05:38,592
Construction fell by 17
per cent and manufacturing

87
00:05:38,672 --> 00:05:43,457
by 7.3 per cent, together
taking 3.6 points off growth,

88
00:05:43,537 --> 00:05:47,075
while finance grew and added 1.6 points.

89
00:05:47,075 --> 00:05:49,400
1998.

90
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:53,272
The Asian financial crisis
began with the collapse

91
00:05:53,352 --> 00:05:58,015
of the Thai baht in July 1997
and spread through currencies,

92
00:05:58,095 --> 00:06:00,625
banks and investors' confidence.

93
00:06:00,625 --> 00:06:04,977
In Singapore the biggest fall
was in finance and insurance,

94
00:06:05,057 --> 00:06:10,762
down 20.2 per cent, which took 2.4 points
off growth, more than the economy's

95
00:06:10,842 --> 00:06:15,575
whole fall of 2.2 per cent, while
manufacturing barely changed.

96
00:06:15,575 --> 00:06:19,600
This was a crisis of
money more than of goods.

97
00:06:19,600 --> 00:06:21,650
2001.

98
00:06:21,650 --> 00:06:26,450
The dot-com bust is the
closest match to the 1930s.

99
00:06:26,450 --> 00:06:33,024
In 2000, electronics made up 69 per cent
of Singapore's non-oil domestic exports,

100
00:06:33,104 --> 00:06:36,225
the goods the island makes for export.

101
00:06:36,225 --> 00:06:40,253
When American technology
spending collapsed,

102
00:06:40,333 --> 00:06:44,081
electronics exports fell by 19.9 per cent

103
00:06:44,161 --> 00:06:48,175
in 2001 and manufacturing
by 11.6 per cent.

104
00:06:48,175 --> 00:06:51,989
Manufacturing alone took
3.0 points off growth,

105
00:06:52,069 --> 00:06:55,300
and the economy shrank by 1.1 per cent.

106
00:06:55,300 --> 00:07:00,100
Chips and computer parts had
taken the place of rubber and tin.

107
00:07:00,100 --> 00:07:03,475
2008 to 2009.

108
00:07:03,475 --> 00:07:08,106
The global financial crisis
began in American banks and

109
00:07:08,186 --> 00:07:12,731
reached Singapore through
trade: transport and storage

110
00:07:12,811 --> 00:07:18,127
fell by 8.8 per cent in 2009 and
manufacturing by 4.2 per cent,

111
00:07:18,207 --> 00:07:22,575
and electronics exports
fell in both 2008 and 2009.

112
00:07:22,575 --> 00:07:27,725
It was partly a crisis of money
and partly a crisis of goods.

113
00:07:27,725 --> 00:07:29,525
2020.

114
00:07:29,525 --> 00:07:32,575
The pandemic followed neither route.

115
00:07:32,575 --> 00:07:36,902
Borders closed and, under the
"circuit breaker" from the

116
00:07:36,982 --> 00:07:41,625
7th of April to the 1st of June
2020, most workplaces shut.

117
00:07:41,625 --> 00:07:44,852
Construction fell by 41.7 per cent,

118
00:07:44,932 --> 00:07:49,955
hotels and food services by
20.2 per cent and business

119
00:07:50,035 --> 00:07:52,412
services by 12.4 per cent,

120
00:07:52,492 --> 00:07:58,350
while manufacturing grew by 7.5
per cent and added 1.5 points.

121
00:07:58,350 --> 00:08:03,350
Taken together, Singapore's crises
have arrived by two main routes.

122
00:08:03,350 --> 00:08:09,448
The goods route, a fall in demand
for what Singapore makes and ships,

123
00:08:09,528 --> 00:08:13,825
was the route of 1930 and
2001 and part of 2009.

124
00:08:13,825 --> 00:08:18,663
The money route, through banks,
currencies and confidence,

125
00:08:18,743 --> 00:08:22,050
was the route of 1998 and part of 2008.

126
00:08:22,050 --> 00:08:27,175
The size of an industry matters
as much as the size of its fall.

127
00:08:27,175 --> 00:08:31,370
Construction's 41.7 per
cent drop in 2020 was the

128
00:08:31,450 --> 00:08:35,383
steepest of any industry
in these five crises,

129
00:08:35,463 --> 00:08:39,658
but construction was then
only about 3.5 per cent

130
00:08:39,738 --> 00:08:43,925
of the economy, so it
cost 1.5 points of growth.

131
00:08:43,925 --> 00:08:48,339
Manufacturing's fall of 11.6
per cent in 2001 was far

132
00:08:48,419 --> 00:08:53,257
smaller in percentage terms,
but manufacturing was about a

133
00:08:53,337 --> 00:08:58,000
quarter of the economy in
2000, and it cost 3.0 points.

134
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,526
An economy that leans
on one large industry

135
00:09:01,606 --> 00:09:04,625
is exposed when that industry falls.

136
00:09:04,625 --> 00:09:10,170
One spread across several industries
of similar size is less exposed,

137
00:09:10,250 --> 00:09:14,735
because a fall in one can be
offset by growth in others,

138
00:09:14,815 --> 00:09:18,729
as finance offset
manufacturing in 1985 and 2001,

139
00:09:18,809 --> 00:09:23,700
and manufacturing offset
construction and transport in 2020.

140
00:09:23,700 --> 00:09:27,912
Diversification does not help
much against a shock that

141
00:09:27,992 --> 00:09:32,675
hits nearly everything at once,
as the lockdown of 2020 did.

142
00:09:32,675 --> 00:09:38,100
Singapore's economy is more
evenly spread than it was in 2000.

143
00:09:38,100 --> 00:09:43,358
Measured at current prices,
manufacturing's share of GDP

144
00:09:43,438 --> 00:09:48,792
fell from 25.9 per cent in
2000 to 17.4 per cent in 2025,

145
00:09:48,872 --> 00:09:54,988
while wholesale and retail trade
grew from 12.5 to 19.7 per cent,

146
00:09:55,068 --> 00:10:00,025
and finance and insurance
from 9.4 to 13.2 per cent.

147
00:10:00,025 --> 00:10:03,550
Electronics matters to Singapore again.

148
00:10:03,550 --> 00:10:07,065
In August 2026 the Ministry
of Trade and Industry,

149
00:10:07,145 --> 00:10:12,674
renamed the Ministry of Energy, Trade
and Industry on the 1st of October 2026,

150
00:10:12,754 --> 00:10:17,780
raised its forecast for growth in
2026 to between 4.5 and 5.5 per cent,

151
00:10:17,860 --> 00:10:22,598
citing "the acceleration in global
AI-related capital expenditure":

152
00:10:22,678 --> 00:10:25,618
spending, much of it in the United States,

153
00:10:25,698 --> 00:10:30,875
on the data centres, chips and equipment
behind artificial intelligence.

154
00:10:30,875 --> 00:10:34,903
The Monetary Authority of
Singapore's policy statement of

155
00:10:34,983 --> 00:10:39,588
July 2026 said that "AI-related
investments have remained robust,

156
00:10:39,668 --> 00:10:43,480
underpinning strong production
and trade of IT-related

157
00:10:43,560 --> 00:10:46,940
goods," and listed as a
risk that "an unexpected

158
00:10:47,020 --> 00:10:51,265
tightening in financial conditions
or pullback in AI-related

159
00:10:51,345 --> 00:10:55,525
investment could impact the
sustainability of GDP growth."

160
00:10:55,525 --> 00:11:00,399
The comparison with 2001 is
natural: growth concentrated in

161
00:11:00,479 --> 00:11:05,600
one product cycle, driven by
American spending on technology.

162
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:09,875
The data also shows how
much has changed since then.

163
00:11:09,875 --> 00:11:15,556
Electronics now makes up about a
quarter of non-oil domestic exports,

164
00:11:15,636 --> 00:11:18,725
against more than two-thirds in 2000.

165
00:11:18,725 --> 00:11:22,447
Electronics exports have
also fallen sharply twice

166
00:11:22,527 --> 00:11:26,025
in recent years without
the economy shrinking.

167
00:11:26,025 --> 00:11:30,136
They dropped by 22.5 per cent in 2019

168
00:11:30,216 --> 00:11:33,421
and by 19.7 per cent in 2023,

169
00:11:33,501 --> 00:11:38,825
while the economy grew
by 1.2 and 1.5 per cent.

170
00:11:38,825 --> 00:11:41,432
A large financial and services sector,

171
00:11:41,512 --> 00:11:45,747
and exports such as pharmaceuticals,
chemicals and machinery,

172
00:11:45,827 --> 00:11:49,575
now carry much of the load
that electronics once did.

173
00:11:49,575 --> 00:11:52,550
What has not changed is the route.

174
00:11:52,550 --> 00:11:56,166
If a shock comes from
technology spending abroad,

175
00:11:56,246 --> 00:12:00,993
it would reach Singapore by the
goods route, the same route that

176
00:12:01,073 --> 00:12:05,750
carried the rubber slump in 1930
and the dot-com bust in 2001.

177
00:12:05,750 --> 00:12:09,328
Where it fits in the bigger
story: The Great Depression

178
00:12:09,408 --> 00:12:13,252
is usually remembered as an
American and European disaster,

179
00:12:13,332 --> 00:12:15,846
but it reached Singapore within months,

180
00:12:15,926 --> 00:12:19,850
through the price of rubber
and the tyres on American cars.

181
00:12:19,850 --> 00:12:23,569
Nearly a century later, the
route a crisis takes into

182
00:12:23,649 --> 00:12:27,950
Singapore still depends on what
the world is buying from it.
